Yes. A CAP/DA beneficiary can transfer, and the governing rule is **Section 3.3, “Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries,”** in NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy **3K-2**. The policy also reinforces the beneficiary’s **right to choose among providers** in the person-centered service plan requirements. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective Sept. 1, 2025, current per amendment check)

## Transfer rule for eligible beneficiaries

Under **Section 3.3**, the **CME must coordinate the transfer to prevent gaps in service provisions**. Before the transfer, the following must be completed:

- determine the anticipated start date of service;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System;
- arrange a home visit within **5 business days** after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence, to assess the environment and identify health and welfare concerns; and
- coordinate services so they **start on the first date of the transfer**. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.3, effective Sept. 1, 2025)

## If the beneficiary is transferring to a different county

Section 3.3 gives more specific rules:

- the transferring CME and receiving CME must coordinate the transfer **on the agreed transfer date or within less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
- both CMEs must discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record must be transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving CME must arrange the home visit within **5 business days** of the move; and
- services must begin on the **first date of transfer into the receiving county**. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.3)

## Beneficiary choice of provider

The policy also says the service plan must ensure the beneficiary’s **right to choose among providers**, documented by a signed **freedom of choice form**. It also requires choice between **CAP/DA, institutionalization, and among Medicaid-enrolled providers**. That supports a beneficiary’s ability to change providers, so long as the transfer is handled under the coordination rules above. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.4.2, effective Sept. 1, 2025)

## Bottom line

A CAP/DA beneficiary **may transfer between providers**, but the transfer is not supposed to be informal or gap-producing. The **CME(s)** must coordinate it, protect health and safety, transfer records, make a timely home visit if the person moves, and ensure services begin without interruption under **Policy 3K-2, Section 3.3**.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and person-centered service plan, and they must be the kind of modification NC Medicaid recognizes as improving accessibility, safety, independence, or community integration. The Multidisciplinary Team must document the need through a recommendation and related supporting records. This is in the current CAP/DA policy effective Sept. 1, 2025, and I did check for amendments; none were returned for the document I cite. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

CAP/DA requires the beneficiary to work collaboratively with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for “equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services.” [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy states that equipment, modification, and technology are intended to:

- improve, maximize, or enhance the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, and independence;
- improve integration into the community;
- improve the beneficiary’s environmental and community accessibility; or
- address 24-hour-a-day, 7-day-a-week coverage concerns. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

For **home modifications specifically**, the policy allows modifications in:

- a **primary private residence** owned by the beneficiary or the beneficiary’s family;
- a **rented residence** if the modifications are **portable**; or
- a **rented residence** if the owner allows the modification. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

Examples of covered home modifications include:

- ramps;
- grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- emergency egress when medically necessary because of physical limitations of the responsible party;
- bathroom accessibility modifications;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- non-skid surfaces;
- door handle and door modifications;
- certain lifts used primarily inside the home;
- smart home devices when the beneficiary lives alone and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan; and
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space removed or damaged during an approved modification. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy also includes related costs as part of the service, such as:

- technical assistance in device selection;
- training in device use by a qualified assistive technology professional;
- purchase costs;
- necessary permits and inspections;
- taxes and delivery charges;
- installation; and
- assessment of the modification by the case manager and any applicable inspectors to verify safety and that it meets the beneficiary’s needs. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

The clearest documentation rule appears in section 5.9.1. For equipment, technology, and **home modifications**, the policy requires:

- **an MDT recommendation** identifying the beneficiary’s needs regarding the home modification being requested;
- that the recommendation **state the cost** of the item or modification required;
- **a training plan** describing how the beneficiary and family will be trained to use the equipment, with completion documented by the case manager and signed by the beneficiary or responsible party;
- a **statement of need in the service plan**;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- **itemized shipping costs** in the proposal request;
- any **other information required for the specific item requested**;
- **quotes**, when required for purchase, adaptation, or modification, in the number NC Medicaid determines is required; and
- written recommendations from appropriate professionals if NC Medicaid or its designee determines they are needed because of special circumstances. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

Supporting CAP/DA text also requires documentation to include:

- a **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, technology, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- the **estimated life of the equipment** and the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit from it; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date the item or modification was provided and the cost, with related charges maintained in the CAP Business system. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Bottom line

For a CAP/DA home modification, the key requirements are that it be for the beneficiary’s primary residence in an allowed setting, be connected to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and service plan, and serve accessibility, safety, independence, community integration, or continuous coverage needs. The MDT’s documentation must show the need, medical support where applicable, cost, service-plan justification, training plan, and any required quotes or professional recommendations. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

