Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet both **provider certification standards** and **ongoing operational/recertification requirements**. A provider can be **decertified** for a wide range of failures, including billing fraud, nonqualified staff, missing training/CEUs, inadequate supervision, deficient documentation, confidentiality violations, failure to correct cited deficiencies, and failure to maintain certification requirements. The current authoritative sources I reviewed show no separate amendment superseding these documents. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment); [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid policy 12B states that an HIV case management provider must:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are provided by **qualified case managers**;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors**;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV case management provider;
- meet **applicable state and federal laws** governing Medicaid participation;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary, operational, and financial records**; and
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed ownership/name/address/telephone/business dissolution changes **within 30 calendar days of the proposed change and no later than five business days after the actual change**. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

In addition, **within one year of enrollment with Medicaid**, the provider must achieve **national accreditation** from one of the designated accrediting bodies: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

### Application/organizational requirements tied to certification

The HIV case management policy also requires, as part of administrative and case management requirements, that the agency submit and maintain things such as:

- list of counties served;
- regular hours of operation;
- emergency after-hours response plan;
- community resource list for the service area;
- articles of incorporation unless a local government unit;
- a **physical business site** verified by site visit, not in a private residence or vehicle;
- organizational chart;
- ownership information for persons with 5% or more ownership;
- required web-based application access;
- business plan and financial information;
- computer capability meeting IT, HIPAA, electronic billing, EFT, and communication requirements;
- compliance with a **precertification onsite visit**;
- written policies on confidentiality, grievances, rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflicts, electronic records, medical records, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation; and
- a **quality improvement plan** addressing outcomes, record review, complaints, utilization/cost studies, claims accuracy, and staff/provider qualifications. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

### Recertification checklist items

The recertification checklist confirms that NC Medicaid expects, at minimum, a recertification submission showing:

- the application is for only **one physical location**;
- signed checklist and required submission format;
- all case managers and supervisors listed with **dates of employment**;
- all counties where HIV case management services are provided;
- all services provided by the agency;
- operating hours and emergency after-hours plan;
- **accrediting agency name and dates** or an explanation;
- frequency of beneficiary satisfaction surveys and how results are used; and
- the numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)

## When a provider can be decertified

Policy 12B says an HIV case management provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a written **plan of correction** within 30 calendar days for unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employment of staff who do not meet policy criteria;
- failure of staff to complete required **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within required time frames;
- failure to provide documentation sufficient to support billing;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid/designated contractor of agency changes, causing inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain certification requirements under Subsection 6.2. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Documentation problems that can lead to risk of decertification

Because the decertification section expressly includes failure to submit required documentation on time and failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing, the documentation rules matter. The policy requires records such as assessments, care plans, progress notes, consent forms, physician/practitioner orders showing diagnosis and medical necessity, documentation of contacts, quarterly care plan updates, and documentation of goal progress. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

It also sets time frames including:

- initial assessment within **five business days** of referral;
- care plan within **five business days** after assessment/reassessment;
- progress notes entered within **24 hours** of contact;
- beneficiary contact within **30 calendar days** of care plan completion, then at least every **three months**;
- care plan review at least every **three months**; and
- annual beneficiary satisfaction survey. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Bottom line

A provider must be properly experienced, staffed, supervised, enrolled site-by-site, accredited, policy-compliant, and able to document and monitor services through an ongoing quality improvement process. Decertification can occur not just for fraud or criminal conduct, but also for operational noncompliance—especially staffing, training, supervision, documentation, correction-plan failures, and failure to maintain the certification standards themselves. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

